# [[-ization|‐ization]], [[-ification|‐ification]]; action of making something that way

====Usage notes====
* When acting as a productive suffix attached to an independent noun, {{m|ja|化|-化}} generally acts as a [[deaccent]]ing suffix. The resulting derived term will typically have a flat ({{m|ja|平板|tr=heiban}}) pitch accent, regardless of the accentuation of the base noun. For example, adding it to the accented noun {{m|ja|グローバル|tr=gurōbaru}} (accent type 2) yields the unaccented compound {{m|ja|グローバル化|tr=gurōbaruka}}.
* In two‐kanji Sino‐Japanese compounds ({{m|ja|[[二字]][[熟語]]|tr=nijijukugo}}), the accentuation depends on morphological transparency and historical origin:
** Transparent derivations and systematic Meiji‐era technical coinages (where the first kanji acts as a distinct base, such as in chemistry) are treated as suffixed words and take a flat accent (e.g., {{m|ja|塩化|tr=enka}}, {{m|ja|悪化|tr=akka}}).
** Older or conceptually fused compounds where it pairs with a bound kanji root are treated as single lexicalized units. These typically default to a head‐high ({{m|ja|[[頭高型]]|tr=atamadaka‐gata}}) accent (e.g. {{m|ja|進化|tr=shinka}}, {{m|ja|美化|tr=bika}}).
* Due to the strong productivity of this suffix, many traditionally head‐high compounds are currently undergoing a historical accent shift ({{m|ja|平板化|tr=heibanka|t=flattening}}) and are increasingly pronounced with a flat accent by modern speakers (e.g. {{m|ja|激化|tr=gekika}}, {{m|ja|強化|tr=kyōka}}).

