====Usage notes====
* When appended as a productive suffix to a freestanding word to form linguistic or literary terminology (such as {{m|ja|名詞句|tr=meishiku}} or {{m|ja|動詞句|tr=dōshiku}}), this term loses its inherent head‐high pitch and dictates that the new pitch drop must fall on the last independent mora ({{m|ja|[[自立]][[拍]]|tr=jiritsu haku|t=independent mora}}) of the preceding word.
*: For example, combining the independent word {{m|ja|アクセント|tr=akusento}} with {{m|ja|句}} yields {{m|ja|アクセント句|tr=akusento-ku}}, shifting the accent nucleus to the fifth mora (ア<span style="border-top: 1px solid;">クセン</span><span style="position: relative; border-top: 1px solid; padding-right: 1px; margin-right: 1px;">ト<span style="position: absolute; top: 0; right: 0; height: 0.35em; border-right: 1px solid;"></span></span>く).
* This suffixing rule does not apply to standard two‐kanji Sino‐Japanese compounds ({{m|ja|[[二字]][[熟語]]|tr=niji jukugo}}), where the first element is a bound morpheme rather than a freestanding word. These deeply fused compounds maintain their own lexical accent patterns, which are frequently accentless (such as {{m|ja|楽句|tr=gakku|t=musical phrase}} or {{m|ja|俳句|tr=haiku|t=haiku}}). It also does not apply to idiomatic derivations (such as {{m|ja|挙げ句|tr=ageku|t=in the end}}).

