==Old Korean==

===Pronunciation===
Conventionally reconstructed as *-s, after the Middle Korean reflexes.

===Etymology 1===
Generally thought to be from an otherwise unattested Old Chinese dialectal pronunciation of the character that began with *s-.

Minority views include that it is a graphic simplification of a different Chinese character, or that it is a ''[[gukja]]'' invented in Korea with [[𠤎]] representing the shape of the tongue while pronouncing /s/ and [[口]] being a radical that denotes a non-standard character.

====Phonogram====
{{head|oko|phonogram|tr=*-s}}

# {{non-gloss|A consonantal [[phonogram]] denoting coda consonant *-s}}

===Etymology 2===

====Alternative forms====
* {{m|oko|之}} {{q|logographic form, in Idu texts}}

====Particle====
{{head|oko|particle|tr=*-s}}

# {{non-gloss|Genitive case marker, chiefly for inanimate nouns.}}

=====Usage notes=====
In Middle Korean, the genitive case marker {{okm-inline|-ㅅ|-s}} was used for both inanimate nouns and honored animate nouns, while the other genitive marker {{okm-inline|-의〮|-úy}} was reserved for non-honored animate nouns.

In the limited Old Korean corpus, {{m|oko||叱|tr=*-s}} appears to be used chiefly for inanimate nouns. Meanwhile, many texts use the genitive {{m|oko|尸|tr=*-lq}}, with no evident Middle Korean reflex, for honored nouns such as the [[Buddha]]. There is a strong argument that {{m|oko|尸|tr=*-lq}} is an allomorphic variant of {{m|oko||叱|tr=*-s}}, representing a phenomenon ancestral to the tensing of the subsequent obstruent that occurred in Middle Korean when {{m|okm|-ㅅ}} occurred between a sonorant and an obstruent. If true, there was no distinction between Old and Middle Korean in the use of the genitive -s.

Unlike the other Old Korean genitive marker {{m|oko|衣|tr=*-uy}}, {{m|oko||叱|tr=*-s}} could follow other case markers.

=====Descendants=====
* {{desc|okm|-ㅅ|tr=-s|pos=genitive case marker for inanimate or honored animate nouns}}
** {{desc|ko|-ㅅ-|pos=interfix}}

====See also====
* {{m|oko|衣|tr=*-uy}} {{q|genitive case marker, often for animate nouns}}
* {{m|oko|尸|tr=*-lq}} {{q|genitive case marker, chiefly for honored animate nouns; possibly an allomorph of 叱}}

